}

Constraints {
  The model is asked to compare findings by whether one engineering fix closes both.
  if (distinct cwe values are not siblings in CWE-89/943/564, CWE-78/77, CWE-22/23/36, CWE-79/80 or CWE-862/285/284) {
    The model is instructed to keep the findings separate even if one control might touch both.
  } else if (one engineering edit fixes both findings) {
    The requested duplicate_of is the LOWER index.
    Eligible descriptions include relabeled defects, shared-helper fixes, global missing controls, cause/effect pairs and shared settings.
    The same-file approximately-30-line criterion additionally requires descriptions clearly concerning one issue; it is not a numeric deduplication guarantee.
  } else { The requested duplicate_of is -1. }
  if (the model is unsure) { It is asked to keep both. }
  for each decision, Constraints {
    independent_sites is requested false when one site fix fixes the others, and true when each would remain broken independently.
    if (duplicate_of == -1) { independent_sites is requested false. }
    This flag controls preservation of extra report locations, not the collapse decision.
  }
}

Constraints {
  The model is assigned exploit-chain strategy, treating supplied TRUE_POSITIVE findings, confidence and CVSS vectors as authoritative; re-verification is excluded by this prompt.
  for each finding, Constraints {
    Requested analysis considers authentication, actual input/output relations, primitives and design controls.
    feeds is requested to include every supplied finding id receiving its primitive, evidenced or not, excluding the same file:line.
    if (a design control genuinely blocks a chain) { The model is asked to explain and downrank it. }
    ranked_findings entries are requested only for a finding that feeds another or whose severity the model is actively changing; each entry has at most two sentences of justification.
    Findings omitted from ranked_findings retain supplied severity according to this prompt; no entry is requested merely to restate an unchanged finding.
  }
  Requested changed-severity bands remain CRITICAL 9.0–10.0, HIGH 7.0–8.9, MEDIUM 4.0–6.9, LOW 0.1–3.9, and INFO for no visible path.
  for each proposed chain, Constraints {
    Every step must supply a value, privilege, reachable state or input the attacker did not already have and the next step needs.
    Preconditions and amplifiers are not requested as chain steps or feeds.
    The named payoff must occur among the steps; otherwise the model is asked to retitle the chain.
    Only finding ids such as f3 may identify findings.
    if (a feeds link has evidence) { The model is asked to include a chain with link_evidence. }
    else { The link stays in feeds without becoming a chain. }
    Inferred AST reachability is weaker evidence; no graph context supplies no flow evidence.
  }
  The model is asked to close these links before writing summary last, describing only listed chains.
}

Constraints {
  The judge is asked to adjudicate only actual response bytes for one static finding, not attacker commentary.
  if (attacked_endpoint cannot plausibly reach the cited sink) { Requested verdict = insufficient_evidence. }
  The model is asked to describe observed bytes before deciding, without claim vocabulary or conclusions.
  mechanism_confirmed and property_violated are requested as separate answers; exploited requires the specific claimed property violation.
  Before exploited, the model is asked to state benign_explanation and identify response bytes excluding it in why_not_benign.
  if (no bytes rule out the benign explanation or the model is unsure) { Requested verdict = insufficient_evidence. }
  if (responses establish neutralization, rejection or withholding of protected data) { The model may return refuted. }
  A mere 200/success body, harmless echo, transport failure, timeout or 401/403/404 does not request confirmation.
  For a missing-authentication claim, measured unauthenticated 2xx service is specifically requested as a violation even with an empty database; separately claimed data disclosure still requires data evidence.
  if (the claim requires another authenticated principal's access) { requires_second_identity is requested true based on the claim's needs. }
  else if (unauthenticated success settles missing authentication) { requires_second_identity is requested false. }
  Deterministic-signal clauses remain both: treat as confirmation unless transcript plainly contradicts it, and the later instruction says the signal IS the ruled-out benign explanation and answer exploited. No unstated priority resolves this tension.
  if (proof compares requests) { control_index must name the other request. } else { control_index is requested null. }
  if (verdict == exploited) { cvss_vector is requested as CVSS 3.1 based on observed impact, not theoretical maximum; plain_consequence must explain the concrete payoff without jargon. }
  else { cvss_vector and plain_consequence are requested null. }
  Requested confidence is integer 0–10 certainty about any verdict, distinct from severity.
  Only the prescribed JSON object is requested, keys in source order beginning observed, verdict, mechanism_confirmed, property_violated and requires_second_identity; detailed field contracts follow below.
}

Constraints {
  The model is assigned structural threat modeling from the supplied snapshot, not a line-specific vulnerability claim.
  The requested stages are ordered: system context, assets with sensitivity, trust boundaries, plausible STRIDE threats per boundary, then open questions.
  for each trust boundary, Constraints {
    The model is asked to name the crossing and reachable assets, walk STRIDE, and emit plausible outcomes with impact and likelihood.
    Prior CVEs raise requested likelihood; supplied design controls lower it.
    Uncertainty alone must not remove a plausible threat; rare remains an available qualitative likelihood.
    At least one threat is requested per boundary, with ids T1, T2, … in emission order; the prompt says caller ranking is deterministic.
  }
  for each supplied MINIMUM BASELINE item, Constraints {
    The model is asked either to base a threat on it with evidence beginning baseline: <ID>, or explain it in open_questions.
  }
  The model is asked to cover FILE INVENTORY directory by directory, including templates, infra/CI, dependency manifests, client code and centralized helpers.
  It must use exact inventory paths, name all implementing/reachable files for each code-backed surface, and avoid unrelated catch-all threats.
  After directory coverage, it is asked to revisit named files and enumerate every evidenced distinct weakness class as a separate threat; sharing a path does not merge classes.
  Before replying, it is asked to subtract all surface_files from the inventory, add missed threats, and report genuinely non-security residual files as unreviewed: <n> file(s) — <reason> in open_questions.
  Only the prescribed JSON object is requested; assets, trust_boundaries and threats must contain objects with the source schema keys, never bare strings.
  The claim that unassigned files never receive later review is retained as prompt rationale, not independently guaranteed by this conversion.
}

Constraints {
  The model is asked to presume each finding wrong until independently confirmed in source; supplied descriptions are claims.
  for each finding, Constraints {
    Requested method: open cited code; walk callers to an external entry or exhaustion; seek refuting controls; probe whether each defence covers every route, including encoded/null/oversized input.
    Requested Glob/Grep searches use narrow repo-relative scopes.
    if (code confirms the defect, a lower-privileged/external actor can reach it, and no control closes the path) { Requested verdict = TRUE_POSITIVE. }
    else if (read evidence establishes a refutation) { Requested verdict = FALSE_POSITIVE, naming the actual control, absent caller or misreading. }
    else { Requested verdict = INCONCLUSIVE; unavailable files and paths leaving the repository remain unresolved. }
    Confidence must score observed evidence: 8–10 means an unsuccessful search for the opposite verdict, 5 or below means guessing. The configured floor is supplied by host code, not invented here.
    Wrong CWE/line on the same real defect requests corrected_cwe/corrected_line_start and retention; the corrected line is the dangerous statement, not a def/class/decorator.
    A genuinely different weakness requests refutation plus additional_findings rather than relabeling.
    if (reframe duty was supplied and only the framing is refuted while the same actor's real exposure is confirmed) {
      Requested verdict = TRUE_POSITIVE with corrected_cwe, reframed true, lower confidence and no confirmed_repeat_lines.
    }
    The reframe exception remains beside the ordinary different-weakness rule; its activation is conditional in build_system_prompt.
    Requested repeats are actually checked identical defects in this file not already listed; distinct additional_findings need the same evidence bar and may be empty.
    if (trait duty was supplied) { control_absence and channel follow schema descriptions; defaults remain when unsure and do not change verdict/confidence. }
    The requested CVSS vector reflects claimed impact even for FALSE_POSITIVE; unjustified metrics remain empty.
  }
  Requested exclusions A–E cover no attacker, no security impact, wrong layer, separately handled issues and noise, with all detailed exceptions retained below.
  In intentionally vulnerable corpora, training intent alone must not activate A/B exclusions; independent refuting reasons still apply.
  Incomplete graph absence alone is not a refutation; when supplied, GraphImpact/GraphNeighbors/GraphCommunityOf guidance differs from GraphImporters.
  Optional graph, trait and reframe clauses are selected by executable assembly, not universal model duties.
}

Constraints {
  The model is asked to infer non-production exclusions from a deterministic structural survey and build/README excerpts, not to inspect an invented source tree.
  Requested exclusion categories include generated code, third-party copies, test-only trees, editor metadata, build outputs, docs-only trees, demo apps, data dumps, non-code localization bundles and non-source lock/manifests.
  if (a candidate is application code, runtime configuration, production IaC, migrations, source API schemas, security code, internally maintained shared libraries or server templates) {
    The model is instructed NEVER to exclude it, even if its directory resembles a normally excluded category.
  }
  if (production status is uncertain) { The model is instructed to retain the candidate. }
  Only one fenced YAML block is requested; executable overlay vetting and keep-ratio reporting are separate source code decisions, not model guarantees.
}

Constraints {
  The model is asked to judge only supplied original confirmation and old/new responses to the same request.
  if (access-control replay has a different or absent original credential) { Requested verdict = unclear. }
  if (a baseline is supplied) {
    It must be read first; a broken/empty baseline prevents silence of the payload from establishing a fix.
    A healthy baseline can rule out the no-data explanation.
  }
  if (a control is supplied) {
    The model is asked to compare both halves; a control changing the same way requests unclear rather than a fix.
    Remediation requires removal of the proof difference while the control retains its behavior.
  }
  Merely losing an old tell, a generic 500, truncation or changed error string does not establish protection.
  Requested remediated requires the new response to show genuine protection; clear persistence supports vulnerable; unresolved evidence or doubt requests unclear.
  Redaction markers mean a value of that kind remains present, not target output or evidence of a fix; masking alone must not establish remediation.
  Only JSON with verdict remediated|vulnerable|unclear and reasoning citing old/new differences in one or two sentences is requested.
}
